<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.14.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00638</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.14.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>046ba798-129c-4c3f-a226-b1fe70c842dd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_74a88f37-794f-47ca-8826-0b457de51fd7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>In questions 1 - 5, determine whether each ordered pair is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>1. \(3x + y > 5\)</para>
            <para>    \(2x − y ≤ 10\)</para>
            <para>  a. (3,−3)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!  (3, -3) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Yes. (3, -3) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
        3(3) + (-3) > 5 &amp;   &amp;2(3) − (-3)≤10 \\
        6>5 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 9≤10 \checkmark
        \end{array}\)</para>
            <para>The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (3, -3) is a solution to the system.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_2abfa603-4fb7-439d-8aa4-313eef64088d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>b. (7,1)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! (7, 1) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is No. (7, 1) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
        3(7) + (1) > 5 &amp;   &amp;2(7) − (1)≤10 \\
        22>5 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 13≤10 ❌
        \end{array}\)</para>
            <para>The point must be true for both inequalities to be a solution to the system. Since (7,1) is not true for both systems, it is not a solution.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_9cacddc8-a918-46c9-99e6-6dfba810ca9f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>2. \( 4x − y &lt; 10\)</para>
            <para>    \(−2x + 2y > −8\)</para>
            <para>  a. (5,−2)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! (5, -2) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is No. (5, -2) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
        4(5) - (-2) &lt; 10 &amp;   &amp;-2(5) + 2(-2)>−8 \\
        22&lt;10 ❌ &amp;   &amp; -14>-8 ❌
        \end{array}\)</para>
            <para> Since (5,-2) is not true for either system, it is not a solution.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_458317ef-7fa0-4860-a797-a2e026c3e5c1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>b. (−1,3)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! (-1, 3) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Yes. (-1, 3) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
        4(-1) - (3) &lt; 10 &amp;   &amp;-2(-1) + 2(3)>−8 \\
        -7&lt;10 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 8>-8 \checkmark
        \end{array}\)</para>
            <para>The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (-1,3) is a solution to the system.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5d5f3bfa-28bc-4f5d-950e-44488d9c37b9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>3. \(y>\frac23x-5\)</para>
            <para>    \(x+\frac12y\leq4\)</para>
            <para>  a. (6, −4)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! (6, -4) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is No. (6, -4) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
        -4 > \frac23(6)-5 &amp;   &amp;6+\frac12(-4)≤4 \\
        -4>-1 ❌ &amp;   &amp; 4≤4 \checkmark
        \end{array}\)</para>
            <para>The point must be true for both inequalities to be a solution to the system. Since (6,-4) is not true for both systems, it is not a solution.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="c3c6b95d-4630-45b9-a90e-ddaec70fec49" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>b. (3, 0)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! (3, 0) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Yes. (3, 0) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
        0 > \frac23(3)-5 &amp;   &amp;3+\frac12(0)≤4 \\
        0>-3 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 3≤4 \checkmark
        \end{array}\)</para>
            <para>The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (3,0) is a solution to the system.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="ed421c51-b59b-4923-a8cf-4508e29ddb1d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>4. \(y&lt;\frac32x+3\)</para>
            <para>    \(\frac34x-2y&lt;5\)</para>
            <para>  a. (−4,−1)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! (-4, -1) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is No. (-4, -1) is not a solution to the system.

     </para>
            <para>When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
        -1 &lt; \frac32(-4)+3 &amp;   &amp;\frac34(-4)-2(-1)&lt;5 \\
        -1&lt;-3 ❌ &amp;   &amp; -1&lt;5 \checkmark
        \end{array}\)</para>
            <para>The point must be true for both inequalities to be a solution to the system. Since (-4,-1) is not true for both systems, it is not a solution.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_140ad5b7-cb3c-41a9-902d-3b47522c825a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>b. (8, 3)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!  (8, 3) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Yes. (8, 3) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
        3 &lt; \frac32(8)+3 &amp;   &amp;\frac34(8)-2(3)&lt;5 \\
        3&lt;15 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 0&lt;5 \checkmark
        \end{array}\)</para>
            <para>The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (8,3) is a solution to the system.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_62942071-43a9-444f-acdd-aa759746db45" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>5. \(6x − 5y &lt; 20\)</para>
            <para>    \(−2x + 7y > −8\)</para>
            <para>  a. (1, −3)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! (1, -3) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is No. (1, -3) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
        6(1)-5(-3) &lt; 20 &amp;   &amp;-2(1)+7(-3)>-8 \\
        21&lt;20 ❌ &amp;   &amp; -23>-8 ❌
        \end{array}\)</para>
            <para> Since (1,-3) is not true for either system, it is not a solution.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e63d490a-6d71-4e3f-84c8-8619dc17c9e7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>b. (−4, 4)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! (-4, 4) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Yes. (-4, 4) is a solution to the system.
     </para>
            <para>When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
        6(-4)-5(4) &lt; 20 &amp;   &amp;-2(-4)+7(4)>-8 \\
        -44&lt;20 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 36>-8 \checkmark
        \end{array}\)</para>
            <para>The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (-4,4) is a solution to the system.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_152c9fb4-de7f-403b-8f8e-a2bee36f959d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>6. Two inequalities are graphed on the same coordinate plane. Which region represents the solution to the system of the two inequalities?</para>
            <para>
              <figure id="fig-00001">
                <media alt="Two intersecting inequalities.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_16_13_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>Region A</para>
            <para>Region B</para>
            <para>Region C</para>
            <para>Region D</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Region D</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.14.3 and 2.14.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Region D. 
        Region D represents the overlap of the graph of the inequality represented by the area shaded blue and the graph of the inequality represented by the area that is striped. 
        Region D represents the overlap of the graph of the inequality represented by the area shaded blue and the graph of the inequality represented by the area that is striped.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
